# FACTORY (the author, 2026-10-05): `@: h f(a)` receives a factory's result; this row wrote `h: f a` until then.
# A method that assigns a free name hands its working value on, through a
# method that only forwards the name, to a held definition that reads it;
# the unit's field is not written. f reads v, a field of the unit, 9. hopf
# calls the held f and only hands v on. From the root: 100 + 9. work adds 2
# to its v and calls hopf: 100 + 11. After it the unit's v is still 9, and
# the root's call gives 109 again. Entry 7.
int: v 9

fn: mk (int: k) fn: () int
    fn: f () int
        return: k + v
    return: f

@: u0 mk(100)

fn: hopf () int
    return: u0()
end: hopf

fn: work () int
    v: v + 2
    return: hopf()
end: work

int: a hopf()
int: b work()
int: c v
int: d hopf()
int: ok 7
if: a != 109 || b != 111 || c != 9 || d != 109
    ok: 81
sendMessage: exit(exit_code: ok; stdout: ""; stderr: "")
return
